  <p>A quick word on the airport transfers. Coming into NYC we took the Uber shuttle which was $22 USD ($30 CAD), but on our way back, due to our poor planning, we couldn't take the shuttle to the airport. To use it you have to walk 23 minutes to the pickup spot and at that point we were too tight on time, so we ended up paying $95 USD ($130 CAD) for an Uber directly from the hotel. The shuttle is still a great option, we just didn't plan properly. Give yourself enough time to get to that pickup spot and you'll be fine.</p>

  <p>A heads up on the exchange rate. We converted $200 CAD through Wise and received $146.51 USD, roughly $1 CAD getting you $0.73 USD. NYC is already an expensive city, and adding the Canadian dollar on top means you feel it with every single transaction. If you don't already use Wise for travel spending, it's worth looking into. Much better rates than exchanging cash at the airport. If you'd like to sign up, feel free to use <a href="https://wise.com/invite/ahpc/julien593" target="_blank" rel="noopener" style="color:var(--denim);">my referral link</a>.</p>

  <p>Would we go back? Maybe. The food scene alone could keep you busy for a month and the energy is unlike anything else. But as a Canadian, every dollar you spend there costs you more than it should.</p>

  <img class="post-img" src="/images/nyc-empire-state.jpg" alt="Empire State Building framed between Midtown buildings" loading="lazy"/>
  <p class="post-img-caption">The Empire State from street level. One of those NYC moments where you just have to look up.</p>

  <p>We also had plans to visit the Brooklyn Bridge and explore DUMBO, something we were genuinely looking forward to, but our flight got cancelled and honestly, that is just travel sometimes. With two kids at home, we had to learn to adapt and adjust our plans. Not everything goes the way you imagined and that is okay. Brooklyn and DUMBO will have to wait for next time. If we do go back, I'd want to explore beyond Midtown. I'd love to spend more time in Brooklyn or even the Bronx, which honestly gets ignored in most travel content. There's a version of New York that feels more real and I haven't found it yet.</p>

  <p>A personal note before I wrap up. We don't do a lot of trips just the two of us, life with two little ones doesn't always make space for that. This one was for our anniversary. No kids, no to-do list, no agenda. I'll be honest though, the mom guilt hit harder on this trip than it did in LA. At least in LA I had the girls around me and their dad was home with our kids, so it felt easier to justify being away. This time it was just us. I found myself checking my phone more, missing the kids more, feeling like I had somehow abandoned them, even though I knew logically that they were completely fine. That feeling is real and I don't think enough people talk about it.</p>

  <p>But I also knew this trip wasn't just good for our relationship, it was good for me. I came home feeling a little more like myself. Sometimes you need to step away to remember who you are outside of the roles you play every single day. NYC gave us that, and that's enough for now.</p>

  <p>That was our weekend in NYC. Short, expensive, and honestly kind of perfect for what we needed it to be.</p>
